      },
      "worked": "For $x^2+x-1\\ne0$, clear the denominator:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-x+1&=A(x^2+x-1)+Bx+C\\\\x^2-x+1&=Ax^2+(A+B)x+(C-A).\\end{align*}$$\nEquate the coefficients of $x^2$, $x$ and the constant terms:\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=1\\\\A+B&=-1\\\\C-A&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting $A=1$ gives $B=-2$ and $C=2$.\nTherefore, $A=1$, $B=-2$ and $C=2$.\n\nThe rational identity is defined for $x\\ne\\frac{-1-\\sqrt5}{2}$ and $x\\ne\\frac{-1+\\sqrt5}{2}$. The cleared polynomial identity holds for all real $x$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
